  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14181368/assets/04676bef8cc37d05e404dc03211968ebaf39f7adbfc54a4a2cb4d5f8cdc3f1a9.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            A circular cake is cut into three slices, as shown above. Given that $m∠A=23z+{4}^{∘},$ $m∠B=12z+{6}^{∘},$ and $m∠C=15z,$ then\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,z=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n${}^{∘}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the entire cake corresponds to the full angle of ${360}^{∘}.$\n\nBy the angle addition postulate,\n\n$$\nm∠A+m∠B+m∠C={360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting the expressions for $m∠A,$ $m∠B,$ and $m∠C,$ and solving for $z,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (23z+{4}^{∘})+(12z+{6}^{∘})+(15z) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 50z+{10}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 50z & ={350}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={7}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
